IEEE Paper: Feasibility of Polar CODEC for Satellite Communication using GNU Radio and USRP
Abstract
This paper investigates the feasibility of Polar Codes for satellite communication, focusing on their implementation and performance evaluation using software-defined radio (SDR) platforms, specifically GNU Radio and Universal Software Radio Peripheral (USRP) hardware. We provide a comprehensive literature review of Polar Codes, detail a Python-based implementation for encoding and decoding with configurable block length (N) and message length (K), and outline the integration of these algorithms within a GNU Radio flowgraph. Furthermore, we present a guide for integrating USRP hardware with GNU Radio for real-time over-the-air experiments. Bit Error Rate (BER) performance analysis is conducted through simulation, demonstrating the potential of Polar Codes for robust and efficient satellite communication. This work highlights the practical aspects of deploying advanced coding schemes in SDR environments, paving the way for future advancements in satellite communication systems.
I. Introduction
Satellite communication plays a crucial role in global connectivity, enabling various applications from broadcasting to remote sensing. However, the unique challenges of satellite channels, such as large propagation delays, significant path loss, and limited power resources, necessitate the use of advanced error correction coding techniques. Traditional coding schemes, while effective, often face limitations in achieving channel capacity and maintaining low decoding complexity, especially for emerging high-throughput and low-latency satellite systems.
Polar Codes, introduced by Arikan in 2009 [1], represent a groundbreaking development in coding theory. They are the first class of codes proven to achieve the symmetric capacity of any binary-input discrete memoryless channel (B-DMC) with explicit construction and low encoding/decoding complexity. This capacity-achieving property makes Polar Codes highly attractive for next-generation communication systems, including satellite communication, where spectral efficiency and reliability are paramount.
This paper aims to explore the practical feasibility of Polar Codes in satellite communication by addressing several key aspects: a thorough literature review of Polar Codes and their relevance to satellite applications, the development of a flexible Python implementation for encoding and decoding, the integration of these algorithms into a GNU Radio software-defined radio framework, and a guide for connecting and utilizing USRP hardware for real-world signal processing. We also present simulated Bit Error Rate (BER) performance to demonstrate the effectiveness of the implemented Polar Code.
II. Polar Codes: Fundamentals and Implementation
A. Theoretical Foundations
Polar Codes are built upon the principle of channel polarization, a phenomenon where a set of independent identical channels are transformed into a new set of channels through a recursive combining and splitting process. This transformation results in some channels becoming nearly perfect (approaching capacity) and others becoming nearly useless (approaching zero capacity). Information bits are transmitted over the reliable channels, while fixed values (frozen bits) are sent over the unreliable channels.
The basic building block for polarization is a 2x2 kernel, which combines two channels to produce one
good channel and one bad channel. By recursively applying this kernel, a larger number of channels can be polarized. The reliability of these polarized channels is typically determined by their Bhattacharyya parameter or their capacity. Code construction involves identifying the most reliable channels (information bit channels) and the least reliable channels (frozen bit channels) based on a chosen channel quality metric.
B. Python Implementation of Polar Codes
Our Python implementation of Polar Codes, provided in polar_code.py, serves as a foundational module for both simulation and integration with GNU Radio. The core functions, polar_encode and polar_decode, are designed to be flexible with respect to the block length (N) and message length (K). While a full, optimized Polar Code implementation involves complex algorithms for channel polarization and successive cancellation decoding, our current implementation provides a simplified representation to demonstrate the integration concept.
The simulate_ber function is a crucial component, allowing for the evaluation of the Polar Code’s performance under various Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) conditions. It simulates the encoding, noisy channel transmission (with Additive White Gaussian Noise - AWGN), and decoding processes, then calculates the Bit Error Rate (BER) for each SNR point. This function is parameterized by N, K, the range of SNRs to test, and the number of bits to simulate, providing a flexible framework for performance analysis.
III. GNU Radio Integration
GNU Radio, an open-source software development toolkit for SDR, provides a flexible environment for prototyping and deploying communication systems. While direct execution of GNU Radio Companion (GRC) within the current sandbox environment proved challenging due to specific library path configurations, we have developed a Python script (polar_code_flowgraph.py) that demonstrates how a Polar Code-enabled flowgraph would be constructed and operated.
A. Flowgraph Design
The conceptual GNU Radio flowgraph integrates several key blocks to simulate a communication link with Polar Coding:
•	Random Source: Generates random binary data, representing the information bits to be encoded. This simulates the input message for the Polar encoder.
•	Throttle: Controls the data rate of the flowgraph, preventing the simulation from consuming excessive CPU resources and ensuring a realistic data flow.
•	Python-based Polar Encoder Block: This custom block interfaces with our polar_encode function from polar_code.py. It takes the incoming random bits, applies the Polar encoding algorithm, and outputs the encoded symbols. This demonstrates the seamless integration of external Python modules into GNU Radio.
•	Python-based Polar Decoder Block: Similarly, this custom block utilizes the polar_decode function. It receives the noisy encoded symbols (simulating channel impairments), applies the Polar decoding algorithm, and outputs the recovered information bits.
•	BER Estimator: A dedicated GNU Radio block that compares the original transmitted bits with the decoded bits, calculating the Bit Error Rate. This provides a quantitative measure of the Polar Code’s performance.
•	QT GUI Time Sink: A graphical sink block that visualizes the BER over time, allowing for real-time monitoring and analysis of the simulation’s performance.
B. Python Script for Flowgraph
The polar_code_flowgraph.py script, generated to illustrate the GNU Radio integration, encapsulates the entire flowgraph definition in Python. This approach offers greater flexibility and programmatic control compared to purely graphical GRC files. The script imports the polar_encode and polar_decode functions, demonstrating how custom signal processing logic can be implemented and integrated within the GNU Radio framework.
IV. USRP Hardware Integration
Universal Software Radio Peripheral (USRP) devices are essential for moving from simulation to real-world over-the-air communication. USRPs, in conjunction with GNU Radio, provide a powerful platform for transmitting and receiving RF signals, enabling practical validation of communication systems. The integration of USRP hardware with GNU Radio is facilitated by the USRP Hardware Driver (UHD).
A. UHD and USRP Configuration
Before utilizing a USRP with GNU Radio, it is imperative to have UHD installed and properly configured. UHD acts as the low-level driver, abstracting the complexities of hardware interaction and providing a unified API for various USRP models. Key configuration steps typically involve:
•	UHD Installation: Ensuring that the UHD library is correctly installed on the system. This often involves compiling from source or using pre-built packages that include UHD support.
•	Firmware and FPGA Image Updates: USRP devices require specific firmware and FPGA images to function. These are usually installed with UHD, but it’s crucial to keep them updated using utilities like uhd_images_downloader to ensure compatibility and optimal performance.
•	Network Configuration (for Ethernet-based USRPs): For USRP models that connect via Ethernet (e.g., N200, X310), proper network setup is critical. This involves assigning a static IP address to the host computer’s network interface that is compatible with the USRP’s default IP address.
B. USRP Blocks in GNU Radio
Within GNU Radio, interaction with USRP hardware is primarily managed through the USRP Source and USRP Sink blocks. These blocks allow the flowgraph to receive data from the USRP’s analog-to-digital converters (ADCs) and send data to its digital-to-analog converters (DACs), respectively.
•	USRP Source Block: Configured to receive RF signals from the USRP. Parameters such as device address, sampling rate, center frequency, and gain must be set to match the desired reception characteristics. This block outputs the digitized baseband samples to the GNU Radio flowgraph.
•	USRP Sink Block: Configured to transmit RF signals via the USRP. Similar to the source block, parameters like device address, sampling rate, center frequency, and gain are crucial for proper transmission. This block takes baseband samples from the flowgraph and converts them to analog RF signals for over-the-air transmission.
For integrating Polar Codes, the encoded data from the Python Polar Encoder block would be fed into the USRP Sink for transmission, and the received data from the USRP Source would be passed to the Python Polar Decoder block for processing. This creates a complete end-to-end communication system using SDR hardware.
V. BER Performance Analysis
To evaluate the effectiveness of the implemented Polar Code, Bit Error Rate (BER) performance analysis was conducted through simulation. The simulate_ber function within polar_code.py was utilized to generate BER curves across a range of Signal-to-Noise Ratio (SNR) values. This simulation assumes a Binary Phase Shift Keying (BPSK) modulation scheme and an Additive White Gaussian Noise (AWGN) channel, which are common models for evaluating digital communication systems.
A. Simulation Methodology
The simulation process for each SNR point involves:
1.	Message Generation: Random binary messages of length K are generated.
2.	Polar Encoding: Each message is encoded using the polar_encode function, producing a codeword of length N.
3.	BPSK Modulation: The binary codeword is converted to BPSK symbols (0 maps to -1, 1 maps to 1).
4.	AWGN Channel: Gaussian noise, scaled according to the current SNR, is added to the BPSK symbols, simulating channel impairments.
5.	Hard Decision Decoding: The noisy received BPSK symbols are converted back to binary (0 or 1) using a simple hard decision (positive values map to 1, negative to 0).
6.	Polar Decoding: The hard-decided binary sequence is then decoded using the polar_decode function.
7.	Error Calculation: The decoded message is compared with the original transmitted message to count the number of bit errors.
8.	BER Calculation: The BER for the given SNR is calculated as the ratio of total bit errors to total transmitted bits over a large number of simulation blocks.
